 {"id": "CF-299", "title": "disk_floor's role-table parity check, as documented, exits 1 on the UNMODIFIED tree — the verification row was asserted, never run", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": "medium", "reasoning": "The command in §3 of module 11 diffs three lines of Python data against six lines of bash case-arm text, so it fails on a clean checkout. It could never have produced the PASS it was recorded with. Worse, tests/test_disk_floor.py:50-53 compares ROLE_DEFAULTS against itself, so changing worker 5 to 7 leaves all 12 tests OK — the parity claim has no gate anywhere. What actually holds is pair parity on (role, GiB) values; a corrected command was written and proven falsifiable in four directions (ladder edit, resolver edit, renamed constant, rewritten case marker).", "acceptance_criteria": "1. A parity check compares parsed (role, value) pairs on both sides and exits 0 on a clean tree.\n2. It is wired into tests/selftest.sh, not left as prose — the test that exists today compares the constant to itself.\n3. Mutating either side turns it red: fixtures for the ladder, the resolver and the constant.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T17:41:16Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T17:41:16Z"}
 {"id": "CF-300", "title": "bunker-crier-connect.sh loses its verdict when HOME is unset, and an inherited FAILED silently flips a PASS to NOT CONNECTED", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": "medium", "reasoning": "Measured: with HOME unset, line 100 errors `HOME: unbound variable` mid-verdict — both remedy lines are lost and the script exits 1 where it should exit 3 (UNVERIFIED). With FAILED=1 inherited from the environment (lib.sh:11 does not reset it), an all-PASS signed run reports NOT CONNECTED (1). Also confirmed while measuring: `b=$(have bunker)` at :79 is dead code because have() ends in `|| true`, so --check can never say 'no bunker client on PATH' — a missing client is misreported as 'FAIL identity — ~/.node/crier.json absent — run --apply'.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. HOME is read with a default and the verdict survives its absence (exit 3, remedies intact).\n2. FAILED is initialised where it is counted, so an inherited value cannot flip a verdict.\n3. A missing bunker client is named as such, and both cases have fixtures.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T17:41:16Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T17:41:16Z"}
 {"id": "CF-301", "title": "check_scan_scope rejects documentation that quotes a non-compliant gitleaks example — the gate cannot tell an example from a claim", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "complexity": "medium", "reasoning": "tests/selftest.sh section 34 runs check_scan_scope.py over the whole repo and FAILs any line mentioning a gitleaks claim without --log-opts. A module ABOUT check_scan_scope.py naturally quotes the bad form as a counter-example, and the scan flags it — measured when a writer documented scripts/check_scan_scope.py and the suite went red for a docs-only diff. The practical result is that the documentation of the gate cannot contain the gate's own failure case, and the cheapest way to make it green is to stop describing it.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. A fenced example explicitly marked as a counter-example is exempt (or the scan skips fenced blocks).\n2. A real unscoped claim in prose still FAILs.\n3. A fixture proves both directions, and the exemption cannot be reached by removing a fence.", "created_at": "2026-11-05T17:41:16Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T17:41:16Z"}
